tal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/e4/e46a5ad8d2f51bc5112c960167879ff352e372bde573834d5394118448a8224c.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $6$ units down to give $y=-2{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{x}-6.$ The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-8)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-6.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a360d7b90f2c2ea9b561ad53626682ed2b2b2f7459ab8c7bdafe8c02486c9c5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f61a7c3-82e6-5f90-8a95-4a79078fd542" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12589fb3-728e-5fe5-802b-ff3319cfa931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/0785d4dd69de876037996489fa0797fa980dfe96d5da33d4563ba8a418dedb65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297636} {:answer/id #uuid "16e8f6eb-84b7-5823-adb4-cf2f87b54568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/db/dbe066f295141ed0c83a61beef5b8408a6cb8840ac20bf5fe33eb62f1e2d5a89.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297650} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4919fd-ff60-570e-ba78-5793ba581c48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/7277d465653d1314448124170c47fa99437e459277788de34b73ecb4011f24f6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297930} {:answer/id #uuid "e5101880-f507-58ce-a125-d07845a33db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d862abef96431d8165d0b6c1fd76419597f828824fab1bf88eb4ccba5e6dfda8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298537} {:answer/id #uuid "ea27864c-1307-5b78-997c-62b24fd008f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/576e9b7a27ea4e85015908217685d69b667db5e84bb2d28cf47b495a7657abc6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5101880-f507-58ce-a125-d07845a33db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d862abef96431d8165d0b6c1fd76419597f828824fab1bf88eb4ccba5e6dfda8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298537} :answer-field/id #uuid "a585ae73-20f0-5b96-bf80-6050c1816cd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f6078fd-cb88-50b6-8d24-b8073d12e091" :question/math-academy-id "q-317541" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-2({3}^{x})+1?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y=2({3}^{x}).$\n\n![](images/7a/7a11b33423337a8d7f0222e1586753d62374f3d8b3eca02cccadd99e50b9eb1b.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-2({3}^{x}).$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,-2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/53/53aedebd12a8f1138efcc9e9815dba7cf15d869da4f321274f6b0cd367e28cac.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $y=-2({3}^{x})+1.$ The point $(0,-2)$ moves to $(0,-1)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/d8/d862abef96431d8165d0b6c1fd76419597f828824fab1bf88eb4ccba5e6dfda8.png)"}]}
